  "notes": "Sheet 0 (Jean-Christophe Quinton, Paris 6e, apartments): the building fills a narrow plot between two older blocks and rises about nine storeys in Vassens limestone (cream, very fine joints); its street front is a cascade of curved, scalloped shells - every storey's bay is a smooth rounded surface stepping back beneath the next, with deep shadows under the curved slab edges and a small round-headed window with a fine metal balustrade in each bay - so the facade is read as stacked sails more than as flat floors; the top storeys step back under a pale roof. The left neighbour is a taller white-rendered block with long horizontal slot windows (sheet 0, bottom right photo); the right is a conventional Paris stone building with balconies and a mansard. Modelled: the limestone block with its storeys stepping back at the street, three windows a storey, rounded corner bays, entrance recess, the set-back top storeys and roof, both neighbours, the street and its plane trees, a paved courtyard behind. Guessed: the plot width (14 m), depth and storey count (read from the elevation photograph), the colour of the roofs and the courtyard. The real curves are doubly curved shells; here each storey is a step with a rounded corner. A block of homes in a city; placed at Paris only."
